<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext (plain HTTP) is permitted, because the server address is chosen at
  runtime and a local one has no certificate to present.

  The three demo tiers reach the server three ways:

    deployed   https://…                 encrypted, and this exemption is unused
    hotspot    http://192.168.x.x:8000   the laptop's own Wi-Fi, no certificate
    USB cable  http://127.0.0.1:8000     through adb reverse, never leaves the wire

  The previous config listed 127.0.0.1, 10.0.2.2 and localhost by name. That
  covered the cable but silently blocked the hotspot tier: a laptop's LAN
  address is different at every venue, and Android's network security config
  matches domains, not ranges, so there is no way to name them in advance. The
  app would have reported "cannot reach the server" for a server that was
  running perfectly (2026-08).

  What this costs, stated plainly: traffic to a local server is not encrypted,
  so anyone already on that Wi-Fi could read it. Accepted because the hotspot is
  created by the laptop for the demo and nobody else is on it, and because the
  alternative — a self-signed certificate the phone must be told to trust — is
  more moving parts to fail in front of a room, for a threat model that is one
  laptop and one phone in the same room.

  NFR-02's encrypted-communication requirement is met by the deployed
  configuration, which is HTTPS and does not use this exemption. Recorded in
  backend/docs/security-audit.md.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
